## Troubleshooting

<PIC:Manual23_131>
{{image description: Troubleshooting table with columns Problem, Possible Cause, and Corrective Action. It covers engine overheating, the malfunction-indicator light coming on, and the starter not cranking. Causes include excessive engine load, low oil, plugged cooling fins, dirty air cleaner, old fuel, fuel-shutoff valve not fully open, discharged battery, blown fuse, engaged blade-control switch, disengaged parking brake, neutral-lock position, empty seat, dead battery, loose electrical connections, and damaged relay or switch; actions include reducing ground speed, adding oil, cleaning air passages or air cleaner, opening the fuel-shutoff valve, charging the battery, replacing fuses, engaging the parking brake, and contacting an Authorized Service Dealer.}}

<PIC:Manual23_132>
{{image description: Troubleshooting table with columns Problem, Possible Cause, and Corrective Action. It covers engine not starting or failing to keep running, engine losing power, machine pulling left or right, machine not driving, and abnormal vibration. Listed causes include empty fuel tank, closed fuel-shutoff valve, low oil, throttle position, dirty fuel filter, stale fuel, dirty air cleaner, seat-switch problems, loose or damaged electrical connections, spark plug issues, excessive engine load, plugged cooling fins, tire pressure, bypass valves, worn pump belt, low or hot hydraulic fluid, bent or unbalanced cutting blades, loose blade mounting bolt, loose engine mounting bolts, damaged engine pulley, bent blade spindle, and loose or worn motor mount.}}

<PIC:Manual23_133>
{{image description: Troubleshooting table with columns Problem, Possible Cause, and Corrective Action. Readable problem headings include cutting height is uneven, blades do not rotate, and clutch does not engage; detailed row text is very small.}}

